---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Process — Omnipeak"
  description="How an Omnipeak project runs, start to finish, with the same person from the first call to handover — and straight answers to the three questions every owner asks before hiring a developer again."
>
  <section class="section page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>How a project runs</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Five steps, one person the whole way through. You'll know the scope, the price, and the
        timeline in writing before anything is built, and you'll own the result.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step">
          <div>
            <h3>Intro call</h3>
            <p>
              Twenty minutes on how your business runs today and where it hurts, with the person
              who would build your system. Plain language, no pitch. If Omnipeak isn't the right
              fit, you'll hear that on this call too.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div>
            <h3>Written proposal</h3>
            <p>
              Exactly what will be built, what it will cost, and when it will be delivered. Fixed
              scope, fixed price. If you're rescuing an existing system, this step starts with a
              written assessment of what you already have.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div>
            <h3>Build</h3>
            <p>
              No account manager in between and no junior doing the real work. Questions get
              answered by the person writing the code, updates come in plain English, and you see
              working software as it takes shape, not a big reveal at the end.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div>
            <h3>Handover</h3>
            <p>
              The system goes live with everything in your name: code, repository, hosting, and
              payment accounts. You get an admin interface you can run yourself, a walkthrough of
              it, and written documentation for whoever comes after.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div>
            <h3>Ongoing support, if you want it</h3>
            <p>
              Omnipeak can keep maintaining and improving the system, with reported bugs tracked
              and handled right away. Or not: there's no fee to keep your own software running,
              and the documentation makes a handoff to anyone else real.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dim">
    <div class="container">
      <SectionHeader
        title="The three questions everyone asks"
        subtitle="Most Omnipeak clients have been burned by a developer before. These deserve direct answers, not reassurance."
      />
      <div class="rows">
        <div class="row">
          <h3>&ldquo;What if you disappear like the last one?&rdquo;</h3>
          <div class="row__body">
            <p>
              Then you lose nothing but time. From day one, the code, repository, hosting, and
              payment accounts are in your name, not held by Omnipeak. The written documentation
              is there so any competent developer could take over. You never depend on one
              person's goodwill.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <h3>&ldquo;Will I be able to run it myself?&rdquo;</h3>
          <div class="row__body">
            <p>
              Yes. That's a deliverable, not a hope. Every system ships with an admin interface
              designed for a non-technical owner, and handover includes a walkthrough of it.
              Changing prices, managing members, and checking your schedule should never require
              a developer.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <h3>&ldquo;What will it cost and how long will it take?&rdquo;</h3>
          <div class="row__body">
            <p>
              You'll know both, in writing, before work begins. Projects are fixed scope with
              defined deliverables, not hourly billing that drifts. If the scope changes
              mid-project, the price change is agreed in writing first.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTA
    title="Start with the twenty-minute call"
    text="The first step costs nothing and commits you to nothing: a plain-language conversation about how your business runs and whether this is the right fit."
    secondaryLabel="See the work"
    secondaryHref="/work/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
